WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.540
In the first film, an open channel spent ion gradients to swing membrane voltage in

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.740
a millisecond. So what opens that gate?

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.960
And how can a second lock or a plug stop ions passing?

4
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.380
A signal acts by opening the gate, so the gate is what makes a channel a receptor.

5
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.300
Gates answer three main keys.

6
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.620
The first key is a chemical ligand.

7
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.640
A neurotransmitter docks outside the channel, twisting the gate open so ions flow.

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.320
Other channels answer messengers inside the cell instead.

9
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:42.233
The second key is membrane voltage itself.

10
00:00:43.953 --> 00:00:48.713
Voltage-gated Na+, K+, and Ca2+ channels carry four voltage sensors.

11
00:00:50.173 --> 00:00:54.373
Each sensor holds a helix called S4, studded with positive charges.

12
00:00:55.913 --> 00:00:58.313
Membrane voltage creates an electric field.

13
00:00:59.653 --> 00:01:04.373
Depolarise the cell, and that field pushes S4 outward, pulling the gate open.

14
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:09.127
That lets voltage-gated Na+ channels feed back on themselves.

15
00:01:10.647 --> 00:01:15.607
Entering Na+ depolarises the membrane further, opening more channels in a fast

16
00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:17.367
pulse, the nerve impulse.

17
00:01:18.727 --> 00:01:20.927
The third key comes from the outside world.

18
00:01:22.147 --> 00:01:26.927
A physical stimulus, such as heat, can open a sensory channel’s gate directly.

19
00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.890
Some receptors conduct strongly only when two events coincide.

20
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:40.190
At synapses, the junctions between nerve cells, the NMDA receptor sits beside AMPA

21
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.190
glutamate receptors.

22
00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.470
With glutamate bound, plus glycine or D-serine, its gate opens.

23
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.670
Yet Mg2+ ions wait outside.

24
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:56.500
Now hold the neuron at rest, electrically negative inside, while glutamate opens

25
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.820
that receptor’s gate.

26
00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.040
Pause and predict.

27
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:04.260
Why does almost no current flow through this open channel?

28
00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:15.933
Mg2+ is positive, so the negative inside pulls it into the open pore, where it

29
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:17.253
sticks and blocks flow.

30
00:02:18.593 --> 00:02:20.893
Now let nearby glutamate receptors open.

31
00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:24.893
Na+ enters and depolarises the membrane.

32
00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:31.033
With the inside less negative, the pull weakens and Mg2+ is driven back out.

33
00:02:32.613 --> 00:02:33.913
Na+ and Ca2+ pour in.

34
00:02:34.993 --> 00:02:40.373
So two events must coincide: glutamate released, and the cell already depolarised.

35
00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:45.267
Because a channel is a hole, toxins and drugs can plug it.

36
00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:52.287
Tetrodotoxin lodges in the outer mouth of nerve and muscle Na+ channels, so Na+

37
00:02:52.287 --> 00:02:54.787
cannot enter and no impulse starts.

38
00:02:56.267 --> 00:03:00.007
Ketamine enters an open NMDA pore to block it from within.

39
00:03:01.267 --> 00:03:03.567
It also makes the channel open less often.

40
00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:08.677
Together, gates and pore blocks turn simple holes into sharp switches.

41
00:03:10.217 --> 00:03:13.417
Ligands, voltage, or physical stimuli open the gate.

42
00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:20.677
Then voltage-sensitive Mg2+, a toxin, or a drug determines whether ions get through.

43
00:03:22.057 --> 00:03:25.577
Signals open the gate, and pore blocks control whether ions pass.
